# The search-task queue, and the novelty grade it orders by.
#
# driver/queuetest.cpp drives rgd::FIFOTaskManager and rgd::PriorityTaskManager
# against a CovManager that answers from a table, and drives
# rgd::SharedMapCovManager against a synthetic branch map and a history map it
# writes itself.  Nothing here compiles or runs a target, so the whole file
# takes milliseconds.
#
# Both halves are scripted for the same reason: neither can be reached from a
# real trace.
#
#   the order   a task is only built for a destination is_branch_interesting()
#               already said yes about -- every ordinary condition sets
#               F_ADD_CONS -- so every task a trace produces scores the same,
#               and a queue that ignored the score entirely would pass any
#               end-to-end test.
#
#   the grade   the three levels come out of the fuzzer's hit-count classes.
#               Reaching kTargetNewClass from a target means arranging a
#               particular history map, a particular loop depth and a
#               particular branch map at once -- three moving parts to observe
#               one function.  Here each is one assignment.
#
# The property that matters most is the one the A/B rests on: with equal scores
# the priority queue must be *exactly* FIFO, bound and all.  Otherwise a
# difference measured in a campaign could be the tiebreak rather than the
# ranking.
#
# RUN: %queuetest --map %t.bmap | FileCheck %s
#
# CHECK: QUEUE-SUMMARY checks={{[0-9]+}} failures=0
# CHECK: QUEUE-RESULT PASS
